  "answer_text": "Great question! Making eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye (also known as strabismus or amblyopia) can feel awkward, but it doesn't have to be. Here are some helpful tips:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f Look at the Eye That's Looking at You\nLook at the eye that is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be rude, it may feel to them as though you are staring at their affected eye.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\udde0 Remember \u2014 They ARE Making Eye Contact with You\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one eye is looking at you and the other is looking somewhere else. That can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact. Keep in mind that both their eyes are, in fact, looking at you \u2014 one of them just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83d\ude0c Don't Overthink It \u2014 Just Carry On\nFor most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you. So don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83e\udee5 Try Softening Your Focus\nIf there's something unusual on someone's face that you don't want to stare at rudely, blurring your eyes slightly lets you look straight at them and listen better. This technique can help you maintain a natural gaze without fixating on either eye.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83d\udd04 It's Okay to Shift Your Focus\nGive yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while. Much like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them and other things about the person start to form your dominant impression.\n\n---\n\n### 6. \ud83d\udde3\ufe0f With Close Friends, You Can Acknowledge It\nIf you can't concentrate because of your friend's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Saying something like *\"Sometimes your eye moves and it throws me off\"* can lighten a possibly awkward situation. However, don't use this approach with a stranger or someone you'll likely never see again \u2014 it's not worth making them feel self-conscious about their situation.\n\n---\n\nThe key takeaway is to **focus on the eye that is directed at you**, stay relaxed, and treat the conversation as you would with anyone else. The person with the lazy eye is almost certainly used to it and just wants to have a normal interaction!",
